  ],
  "text": "A dolphin cannot fall fully asleep. If it did, it would drown. Breathing is a decision for a dolphin, not a reflex. Stop deciding and it stops. So it sleeps one half of its brain at a time. One hemisphere rests. The other stays alert, and the eye on that side stays open. Then they swap. It never gets a full night, and it never stops. Evolution had every reason to simply drop sleep here, and it did not. It bent the entire design around keeping it instead. Ducks do a version of it. A row sleeps together and the outer birds keep one eye open. Frigatebirds sleep while flying. Giraffes manage two hours. Some bats take nineteen. The amount changes everywhere you look. The requirement never does. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
